# Regression test: `exit` must succeed normally after a job was `terminate`d, even
# though `terminate` leaves a one-time "Terminated" snapshot behind for `jobs` to report
# (see `tests/terminated_job_is_reaped.crush`).
#
# Root cause: `GlobalState::jobs()` used to hand back that snapshot row to *every*
# caller, not just the `jobs` builtin -- including `exit`'s own `random_other_job` check
# (src/builtins/control/mod.rs), which treats any row `jobs()` returns as "another job
# still running" and refuses to exit ("There are running jobs.") unless `--force` is
# given. That made a single terminated job -- long after its process was actually
# gone -- block the *next* `exit` call, even with no snapshot ever consumed via a `jobs`
# listing in between.
#
# Fixed by splitting `GlobalState::jobs()` (live jobs only -- used by `exit`, `timeout`,
# and everything else that needs to know whether work is still genuinely running) from
# `GlobalState::jobs_for_listing()` (used only by the `jobs` builtin itself, which is the
# one place that should still report a job that was just terminated).
# `exit`'s own "are there other jobs" check (`GlobalState::jobs()`) only excludes a job
# once its `JobControlData` has actually been dropped -- a job whose worker thread
# hasn't yet finished returning from `cmd.rs` (after observing the SIGKILL'd child's
# death) still resolves via `job_control_data.upgrade()` and reads as "still there"
# even though its live status already says Terminated (confirmed directly: removing
# this wait entirely makes `exit` fail with "There are running jobs." on nearly every
# run). Poll until it's actually reaped instead of guessing a fixed delay.
#
# Matched by a distinctive sleep duration rather than by id: ids get recycled (see
# tests/job_orphaning_on_parent_death.crush), and this very polling loop creates new
# jobs of its own on every iteration -- one of which could plausibly be handed
# $job_id's own freed id, making the filter match the loop's own still-running job
# forever instead of correctly reaching zero.
$job_id := $(cmd sleep 8171 &)
terminate $job_id

$tries := 0
while {(($tries < 20) and (($(jobs | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 8171"} | count)) > 0))} {
    sleep 50ms
    $tries = ($tries + 1)
}
assert (($(jobs | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 8171"} | count)) == 0) "expected the terminated sleep job to be fully reaped within the retry budget"

exit
